WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:33.583
Identify the record home. A request carries a tenant or entity key. The routing rule maps that key to an authoritative shard before data is read or written.

2
00:00:33.583 --> 00:01:06.750
Distribute independent records. Different record groups live on different database shards. This can spread storage and write work when the groups are actually independent enough.

3
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:47.333
Find a hot key. One popular event can overload its shard even when every shard has the same row count. Workload distribution matters more than counting keys.

4
00:01:47.333 --> 00:02:21.250
Examine a global query. A query across all events may contact every shard and merge results. Cross-shard joins, transactions and uniqueness require deliberate support or redesign.

5
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:57.667
Move ownership safely. Rebalancing needs copying, verification, a write/cutover policy and an updated routing map. Changing a modulo number does not move existing records.

6
00:02:57.667 --> 00:03:42.500
Choose the simplest fit. Shard only when requirements and measurements justify the coordination cost. A suitable index or larger database may solve the current problem with much less complexity.
